WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.450
Lab 1 asks you for two things. This video
shows the first one.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.900
Nothing here needs an API key. It all runs
offline.

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:12.400
You are not graded on whether every check
passed.

4
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.000
You are graded on running it and showing
the result.

5
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.794
Two failed checks with honest pasted

6
00:00:18.794 --> 00:00:21.850
output beat a submission with nothing
pasted.

7
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.875
Run python verify env dot p y from the
folder that holds it.

8
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:31.350
The full output runs past one screen, so
this shows the top.

9
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.275
Nine checks print, one per line, each with
a symbol.

10
00:00:35.625 --> 00:00:40.225
Python version passes. Then the virtual
environment check warns.

11
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:43.600
It says you are not in a virtual
environment.

12
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.475
A warning is not a failure, and the lab
still runs.

13
00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:52.475
The arrow under it prints the fix, python
dash m venv dot venv.

14
00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:56.200
Then you activate it with the line for
your own system.

15
00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.225
The other seven checks all pass here.

16
00:00:59.575 --> 00:01:03.050
Packages, the settings file, and no key
stored in it.

17
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.375
Your canary tokens, the target files, and
saving a file.

18
00:01:07.725 --> 00:01:11.675
The last check runs the agent, and it
answered in one step.

19
00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:16.125
Now save the output, because the
assignment asks you to paste it.

20
00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:20.175
The redirect writes it to a file, so
nothing prints here.

21
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:24.300
Then read the end of that file, which is
the part you need.

22
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.275
Everything passed, and then your Module 0
Gate code.

23
00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:31.700
The pipe into sed hides my code on
purpose.

24
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.875
Yours comes from your student ID, so no
two are the same.

25
00:01:36.225 --> 00:01:40.350
Copy the whole line, including the letter
after the last hyphen.

26
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.875
Paste it into the Module 0 Gate in Canvas.

27
00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:48.550
It is due on day 3, and it is your
attendance check for the term.

28
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.275
The last lines look ahead to week 2, when
you have an API key.

29
00:01:53.625 --> 00:01:58.200
Run this again with dash dash live and
submit the code ending in L.

30
00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:03.150
Here is the failure that costs the most
time and is not a real failure.

31
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.575
The same command, run from the wrong
folder.

32
00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:10.900
Python cannot find the file, and it prints
the path it tried.

33
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.150
Move back one folder and run it again.

34
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.125
It works, and the check starts printing.

35
00:02:17.475 --> 00:02:21.325
If a command cannot find something, check
your folder first.

36
00:02:21.675 --> 00:02:25.075
Nine times out of ten, that is the whole
problem.

37
00:02:25.425 --> 00:02:30.025
One more command. Run python dash m common
dot canary.

38
00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:34.525
It prints three tokens. Sysprompt,
customer, and kb.

39
00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:38.750
The dash m flag runs a module by its
dotted name.

40
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.600
That flag is what makes the lab import
lines work.

41
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.693
Your three tokens are built from your
student

42
00:02:45.693 --> 00:02:48.375
ID, so a classmate has three different
ones.

43
00:02:48.725 --> 00:02:52.075
You will hunt these tokens as evidence in
Week 5.

44
00:02:52.425 --> 00:02:56.375
Two easy ways to lose points here, and
both are avoidable.

45
00:02:56.725 --> 00:02:59.025
The first one is predicting late.

46
00:02:59.375 --> 00:03:02.950
Running the code first, then writing that
you predicted it.

47
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.425
That is the one thing this lab is built to
catch.

48
00:03:06.775 --> 00:03:10.450
Predicting badly is fine. Predicting late
is not.

49
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.975
A wrong prediction you explain in one
sentence earns full credit.

50
00:03:15.325 --> 00:03:19.200
The second one is describing output
instead of pasting it.

51
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.550
Several graded rows are the paste itself.

52
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.625
One file, four parts, in this order.

53
00:03:25.975 --> 00:03:28.825
Your environment check output, then your
five

54
00:03:28.825 --> 00:03:31.675
predictions with reasons and the real
output.

55
00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:35.725
Then your dictionary output, then your
three written answers.

56
00:03:36.075 --> 00:03:39.025
That order is the order the grader reads.

57
00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:42.565
Do not upload the two python files.
Everything

58
00:03:42.565 --> 00:03:45.825
the grader needs is pasted inside the one
file.

59
00:03:46.175 --> 00:03:48.721
If setup fights you for thirty minutes,

60
00:03:48.721 --> 00:03:51.725
post the whole output and start Part 2
anyway.
